REGIONAL REGULATION OF PULANG PISAU REGENCY

NUMBER 6 OF 2024

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF PULANG PISAU,

Considering:

a. that protection of women and children is an integral part of human rights that must be guaranteed, protected, and respected by the state;
b. that violence against women and children in the Region tends to increase, therefore the Regional Government and/or community need to play an active role optimally to provide protection, so that women and children are prevented and freed from violence or threats of violence, torture, or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth;
c. that protection of women and children is a mandatory concurrent affair that is the authority, obligation, and responsibility of the Regional Government, thus requiring regulation as the basis for implementing comprehensive and integrated protection of women and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019) as amended by Law Number 16 of 2019 concerning Amendment to Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 186, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6401);
3. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 3143);
4. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3277);
5. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 138 Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
6. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
7. Law Number 5 of 2002 concerning Establishment of Katingan Regency, Seruyan Regency, Sukamara Regency, Lamandau Regency, Gunung Mas Regency, Pulang Pisau Regency, Murung Raya Regency and Barito Timur Regency - 3 - in Central Kalimantan Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 18 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4180);
8. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 1 of 2023 concerning the Criminal Code (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6842);
9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
10. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 01 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Empowerment of Child Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 42);
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903;
14. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 4 of 2018 concerning Guidelines for Establishment of Regional Technical Implementation Units for PPA (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2018 Number 532);
15. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2022 concerning Service Standards for Protection of Women and Children (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 85;
16. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2023 concerning Implementation of Concurrent Government Affairs in the Field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 175);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PULANG PISAU REGENCY

and

REGENT OF PULANG PISAU

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

Article 2

The implementation of PPA is carried out based on the principles of:
a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. protection;
d. best interests of women and children; and
e. non-discrimination.

Article 3

The regulation of the basic principles of PPA implementation aims to:
a. prevent acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity of women and children;
b. eliminate all forms of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity of women and children;
c. protect, provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Article 4

Women and children who are victims of acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity shall receive the following rights:
a. Right to guarantee space for community participation in government, including in decision-making/policy-making;
b. Right to population documents and civil registration;
c. Right to Legal Aid;
d. Right to be respected for their dignity and worth as human beings;
e. Right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
f. Right to determine their own decisions;
g. Right to obtain information;
h. Right to confidentiality;
i. Right to compensation;
j. Right to social rehabilitation;
k. Right to complaint handling;
l. Right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and
m. Right to assistance.

Article 5

Child victims of acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 4, shall also receive special rights, as follows:
a. Right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. Right to basic services;
c. Right to equal protection;
d. Right to be free from various stigma;
e. Right to employment;
f. Right to decent housing and environment;
g. Right to Women's empowerment; and
h. Right to prevention of violence.

CHAPTER III

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 6

Obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children are the shared responsibility of:
a. regional government;
b. community;
c. family;
d. parents; and
e. non-governmental institutions including Customary Institutions and Business Entities.

Article 7

The obligations and responsibilities of regional government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include:
a. implementing protection policies for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity as determined by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity for women and children according to regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation and protection of women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades their dignity and humanity.

Article 10

1. The obligations and responsibilities of Customary Institutions in the implementation of protection for women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter e, include:
a. customary institutions have the responsibility to uphold the dignity and human rights of women and children in accordance with highly valued humanitarian values;
b. responsible for preventing all forms of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children within the customary environment;
c. ensuring gender equality and children's rights are respected, without discrimination based on gender or age;
d. responsible for raising awareness among customary communities regarding the rights of women and children, including awareness of the importance of protection and respect for their rights;
e. organizing outreach programs and training for members of customary communities, especially customary leaders, about the rights of women and children and effective protection methods;
f. providing psychosocial and legal support to victims of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children including facilitating victims' access to protection and legal institutions;
g. playing an active role in the reporting process and law enforcement regarding cases of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children in customary communities;
h. facilitating the establishment of PPA institutions at the customary level tasked with protecting their rights and providing assistance and support to victims;
i. integrating customary values that support equality and PPA into community outreach and education programs; and
j. building partnerships with non-governmental organizations, protection institutions, and educational institutions to strengthen PPA efforts.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of Business Entities in the implementation of protection for women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter e, include:
a. obligated to comply with all laws, regulations, and rules related to PPA at the national and regional levels;
b. providing a safe and supportive work environment, free from harassment, discrimination, violence, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children;
c. providing shelter for female workers in the workplace;
d. forming partnerships with regional governments in PPA;
e. providing empowerment for women and children as a shared responsibility with regional governments;
f. conducting outreach programs in communities around the company to raise awareness about PPA;
g. creating policies, programs, and PPA activities;
h. supporting social programs that enhance women's economic independence and support children's education;
i. supporting the establishment of protection centers and services for victims of violence against women and children; and
j. supporting PPA programs in Pulang Pisau Regency through Corporate Social Responsibility instruments and/or assistance in other forms in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IV

DUTIES OF REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 11

The Regional Government is tasked with carrying out prevention, handling and recovery efforts for victims of violence against Women and Children, by:
a. collecting data and information about Women and Children Victims of acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to PPA;
c. providing education about anti-violence values in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor against women and children;
d. preventing child marriage;
e. providing rehabilitation facilities; and
f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of PPA against acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor.

Article 15

The implementation of services for victims is carried out with the principles of:
a. no fees charged;
b. fast;
c. safe;
d. empathy;
e. non-discrimination;
f. easily accessible; and
g. confidentiality.

CHAPTER VI

COOPERATION AND PARTNERSHIP

Article 16

1. In order to achieve the objectives of implementing PPA as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with:
a. the Provincial government;
b. other Regency/City governments;
c. the National Police;
d. the Prosecutor's Office;
e. the Court; and/or
f. non-governmental institutions.
2. The cooperation referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. provision of evidence and witnesses;
e. follow-up in accordance with applicable law; and
f. prevention efforts to prevent the occurrence of criminal acts against Women and children.
3. The cooperation referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a joint agreement.
